  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The cold front that moved across central NC has \nstalled across the eastern Coastal Plain. Drier air will continue to \nmove into the region from the northwest but lingering moisture near \nthe front could lead to a stray shower or storm this afternoon along \nthe Coastal Plain.\n\nThe latest surface analysis shows the slow moving cold front now \nlocated across the eastern Coastal Plain with northwest winds across \nall of the area including at Fayetteville, Goldsboro and Clinton. \nDrier air has been filtering into the region thanks to a northwest \nor westerly flow in the low levels. The RAOB at KGSO this morning \nnoted a PW of only 0.94 inches which is around the 18th percentile. \nThe drier air combined with good mixing has allowed dew points to \nfall into the upper 50s at KINT, KGSO, KEXX, and KHNZ and the lower \n60s across most of the remainder of the Piedmont. Further east and \nsoutheast across the Coastal Plain and Sandhills moisture lingers \nwith dew points in the upper 60s to around 70. This has resulted in \na bit more cumulus clouds and perhaps a little vertical growth near \nand just behind the front from Maxton to just southeast of \nFayetteville to just south of Goldsboro. Wouldn't be shocked if a \nrogue shower or storm develop near or east of this line through \naround 6pm but the coverage and intensity should be very limited and \nmore favored to the east.  \n\nGenerally fair weather is expected overnight with a northwest to \nwesterly flow aloft, An approaching mid level disturbance may \ntrigger a weak area of low pressure to develop in the Carolinas \novernight on the front, or at least buckle the front and the \nmoisture back west slightly. Some of the CAMs, primarily the HRRR \nsuggest a rogue shower could develop late tonight across southern or \nsoutheastern areas, there is very little support for this in the \nother CAM guidance so will exclude any overnight PoPs. There could \nbe some patchy fog or low stratus across the Sandhills and southern \nCoastal Plain toward day break as well. Lows tonight should range in \nthe lower 60s in the Triad and VA border counties, with mid 60s in \nthe Triangle and southern Piedmont and the upper 60s in the \nSandhills and Coastal Plain. Could see a few upper 50s in the \ntypically colder spots such as Henderson and Sanford. These lows \nrange from 1 to 4 degrees below average. Even more notable will be \nthe dewpoints in the mid 50s to mid 60s which will result in a much \nmore pleasant morning for the first day of school in many locations.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... There will be a slight chance for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday afternoon along and east of the I-95 corridor.\n\nThe cold front moving across central North Carolina today will \nbecome hung up along the Atlantic coastline tonight into Tuesday. \nThe bulk of the convection that will occur Monday will be along the \nimmediate coastline, but models have inched the western edge of the \nprecipitation slightly farther to the west than they were yesterday. \nOther than around Fayetteville, it still appears that nearly all \nprecipitation that occurs should remain east of I-95, and chances \nfor storms are anywhere between 20-40%. The Storm Prediction Center \ndoes have a marginal (level 1 of 5) risk for severe storms along the \ncoastline, just clipping the southern tip of Sampson County. Again, \nit is along the coastline where the greatest instability and wind \nshear will remain.\n\n\nKey Message 3: Diurnally based convection returns to the forecast on \nWednesday, maximized on Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... The front will inch northward Wednesday and \nThursday, eventually extending from Elizabeth City to Monroe. The \nchance of showers/storms Wednesday afternoon should generally be to \nthe southeast of the Triangle, although all locations are likely to \nsee at least some minimal precipitation Thursday. As the front \nretreats back to the coastline for the weekend, precipitation will \nbecome more scattered, but locations in the southeast will have \nhigher chances of rain than locations in the northwest. Temperatures \nshould be near normal for late August, with highs around 90 and lows \naround 70.",
